# 097 · Give control a kit bundle from the dashboard

**Status:** `PROPOSED` · **Version:** [v0.1.4](../roadmap.md#v014)

## What

The dashboard takes a kit repository written elsewhere, as a git bundle file, as [096](_completed/096-kits-from-elsewhere.goal) lets REST, MCP and the command line do today. The owner picks the file, names the kit, and sees what control kept: the head commit, the number of commits, the kit's name and target, or the refusal in words. Control keeps every commit with its id, so a contract that names a commit still matches.

An agent that works through MCP gets a way to send a bundle that is not one long text argument: for example, a short-lived upload link that control makes for one bundle, which the agent's own tools can send the file to.

## Why

On 2026-10-22 the owner gave control the kit of a campaign written elsewhere. The dashboard had no place for a bundle: its **New campaign** page takes kit folders, which make new commits. The command line needs a token file, which the owner did not know about: "I don't know what token file is or where it is". MCP's `kit_add` takes the bundle as base64 inside the call, which is impractical for an agent to write out for a bundle of half a megabyte. So the kit was sent with a script that called `kit_add` with the file. Earlier the same day the owner asked: "I thought we already had an upload kit thing?"

## Done when

- [ ] A mockup, approved by the owner, of where the upload sits and what it says.
- [ ] The upload on the dashboard, with the same checks and refusals as `kit_add`, and a test.
- [ ] A way for an agent over MCP to send a large bundle without writing it out as text, and a test.
- [ ] The pages that describe kits kept on control.

## Decisions

- 2026-10-24, owner: v0.1.1 becomes the code clean-up; this goal moves to v0.1.3.

## Open questions

- Where the upload sits: on **New campaign**, beside the kit folder upload, or on a page that lists the kits control keeps.

## Progress

- 2026-10-22: Goal written from the owner's first kit given to control from elsewhere.

